#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Welcome to *Use a quantum computer today*! The goal of this course is for "
"you to run code on a real quantum computer in the shortest time possible, "
"with no prior background required. Let's get started.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This first lesson is a gentle, hands-on introduction to quantum computing "
"that works for curious beginners and busy leaders alike. You'll learn about "
"quantum circuits and write a small quantum program that creates "
"**entanglement**, and run it on a real IBM&reg; quantum computer. You also "
"have the option to run the same program on a simulator, should you choose to"
" skip running on a real quantum computer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"You can run this notebook from top to bottom in a fresh Google Colab "
"runtime, or run locally.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Set up {#set-up}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To run this notebook in Google Colab, you'll need a **Google account** so "
"you can open notebooks and execute cells in the browser.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To run on a real IBM quantum computer, you'll also need a (free) [**IBM "
"Quantum&reg; Platform "
"account**](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/signin?redirectTo=%2F). You can "
"open an instance using the Open Plan to get 10 minutes of quantum processor "
"unit (QPU) time per 28-day rolling window, which actually is quite a lot! "
"*If you have trouble with your account, see the [Support "
"page](/guides/support).*\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"You can also run this notebook locally after installing Qiskit by following "
"the instructions on [**IBM Quantum Platform**](/guides/install-qiskit).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Installation and imports {#installation-and-imports}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In Colab, we install dependencies inside the notebook so everyone runs the "
"same tools. The next cell installs Qiskit with the [visualization "
"module](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/visualization) plus two add-"
"ons: **Aer** (fast simulators) and the **IBM Runtime** client (for quantum "
"computer runs).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Next, we have a number of imports. The `QuantumCircuit` class is where we "
"define our quantum bits, or qubits, and define operations on those qubits. "
"This is our first quantum term: a **qubit** is the basic building block of "
"quantum computation, just as a bit is the building block of classical "
"computation. We'll learn more about the special properties of qubits as we "
"create our circuit. Next, `plot_histogram` will be used to visualize the "
"results of our quantum circuit. `AerSimulator`, allows us to simulate the "
"quantum circuit on a classical computer. But simulators cannot run quantum "
"circuits at the same scale as real quantum computers; that's why we need "
"real quantum computers. This can be helpful for testing, debugging, or "
"educational purposes; or if you've exhausted your 10 free minutes of QPU "
"time. The `preset_passmanagers` help optimize the circuits to run "
"efficiently on hardware, which becomes really important as our circuits "
"become more complex. `Sampler` and `QiskitRuntimeService` are necessary to "
"actually run the circuit on the quantum computer. More on this later.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Next we'll do a quick environment check. We confirm versions and confirm "
"imports.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"If anything fails here, it's usually a dependency install issue; fixing it "
"now prevents confusing errors later.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A brief side quest: use the Composer {#a-brief-side-quest-use-the-composer}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Before we write code, it's helpful to see circuits visually. IBM Quantum "
"**Composer** lets you build circuits by dragging gates onto wires. It's a "
"great way to learn what a circuit is doing without getting distracted by "
"syntax.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Open the Composer [here](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/composer).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Once it loads, launch the guided tutorial from the menu: **Help | Build your"
" first circuit**. Work through it at your own pace. As you go, pay attention"
" to how each gate changes what you expect to measure.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The tutorial steps through building the \"Hello World\" circuit. It's "
"visualized in what is called a circuit diagram, where the qubits are "
"represented by horizontal lines and the gates acting on those qubits are "
"represented by boxes or other symbols on the lines. This circuit introduces "
"us to a few key features of qubits and quantum computers:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"First, the red box labeled \"H\" is a Hadamard gate, which creates a "
"**superposition state** of qubit 0. Unlike a bit, which can only be in the "
"state 1 or 0, a qubit state can include both possibilities at once, with "
"certain weights (called amplitudes) attached to each. Superposition doesn't "
"mean you'll ever see both results in one measurement; it means the state is "
"set up so that either outcome can happen when you measure.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Second, the circles and vertical line connecting the two qubits is a CNOT "
"gate, which generates **entanglement** between the two qubits. Entanglement "
"is a special kind of link between qubits. When qubits are entangled, the "
"outcomes of measurements can be strongly correlated in a way that doesn't "
"match what we'd expect from ordinary independent coin flips, or from any "
"classical correlation. When two qubits are entangled, measuring one will "
"instantaneously tell you the outcome of measuring the other.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Another key idea you'll see in the tutorial is **shots**. Because "
"**measurement** is the moment a qubit stops behaving like a quantum state "
"and gives you a classical readout, a superposition state will "
"probabilistically collapse to either a 0 or a 1 when it's measured. So, to "
"learn about that superposition, you need to measure it many times by running"
" the same circuit to accumulate statistics. These are called shots.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Think about each question below, then click to reveal the answer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Can you see a superposition directly from a single measurement?\n"
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Answer\">\n"
"No. A single measurement always returns a classical value: either 0 or 1. The \"mixture\" idea shows up only in the statistics you see after many runs, also called shots.\n"
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In plain language, what does entanglement give you?\n"
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Answer\">\n"
"It gives you linked outcomes. Measuring one qubit tells you something about the other. This link is stronger than independent randomness and stronger than any purely classical correlation or shared randomness can explain.\n"
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"If a qubit is in a superposition, what do you see in a single measurement, and why do you need many shots?\n"
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Answer\">\n"
"In a single measurement you see only one classical result: either 0 or 1. You need many shots because the \"superposition\" shows up as a probability distribution, which you can only estimate by collecting statistics over repeated runs.\n"
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Look at the measurement histogram of your Hello World circuit in the Composer. What do you see? Why is this a signature of entanglement?\n"
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Answer\">\n"
"It shows you a 50% chance of being in the state $|00\\rangle$ and a 50% chance of being in the state $|11\\rangle$. This means that if you measure one to be 0, the other will be 0 and if you measure one to be 1, the other will also be 1. This is a correlation between the two qubits that can be explained by entanglement.\n"
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Create and run a quantum program using Qiskit {#create-and-run-a-quantum-"
"program-using-qiskit}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Okay, back to coding. We're going to generate the same entangled state as we"
" did in Composer (called the **$\\Phi^+$ (phi-plus) Bell state**) but this "
"time, we'll write the code by hand. We need to build this skill because as "
"we scale to more qubits and more complicated circuits, the Composer won't be"
" able to help us.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To build the Bell state circuit, we use an $H$ (Hadamard) gate to put the "
"first qubit into an equal superposition. Then we apply a $CX$ (controlled-"
"NOT) gate, which entangles the two states together. The two qubits are now "
"correlated in a way that has no classical equivalent.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/use-a-qc-"
"today/build-and-run-your-first-quantum-program/extracted-"
"outputs/49062fe9-3345-4923-88cb-5f5efbfd9376-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Helper function for running circuits {#helper-function-for-running-circuits}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now let's define a helper function that handles the process of running a "
"quantum circuit and retrieving measurement results. This function takes care"
" of transpiling the circuit to match the backend's instruction set, running "
"it through a `Sampler` primitive, and extracting the counts from the "
"results.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Run on a QPU and visualize results {#run-on-a-qpu-and-visualize-results}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Finally, we run the circuit on an IBM quantum processing unit (QPU) in the "
"cloud for 1000 shots and plot the results. IBM QPUs are physical systems "
"that can pick up noise, so gates are slightly imperfect, measurements can be"
" wrong sometimes, and device calibration drifts over time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Running on real quantum computers also introduces practical considerations. "
"Jobs can sit in a queue, because many people may be using the same device. "
"You also have to choose a shot count that balances statistical "
"considerations (more shots equals a higher signal-to-noise ratio) with "
"time/cost constraints.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Follow the instructions in the code comments of the next cell. After running"
" the cell, you should see a histogram with approximately equal counts for "
"the $00$ and $11$ bitstrings, with some occurrences of $01$ or $10$ due to "
"noise. The following cell in this notebook runs the same circuit on a "
"simulator, should you choose to skip running on a QPU.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/use-a-qc-"
"today/build-and-run-your-first-quantum-program/extracted-"
"outputs/c973f036-685c-4612-a021-bb6866a679f3-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Run on a simulator and visualize results {#run-on-a-simulator-and-visualize-"
"results}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A simulator is a \"perfect world\" version of quantum computing. Here we run"
" the circuit on a simulator for 1000 shots and plot the results. You should "
"see approximately equal counts for the $00$ and $11$ states, with no "
"occurrences of $01$ or $10$, which is the signature of the Bell state's "
"perfect correlation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/use-a-qc-"
"today/build-and-run-your-first-quantum-program/extracted-"
"outputs/8c8d63cd-c409-415b-abc6-8875bf136479-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding-1}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "What are the two gates that create the Bell state here?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Answer\">\n"
"An H gate on qubit 0, followed by a CX gate with qubit 0 as control and qubit 1 as target.\n"
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"On an ideal simulator, which two bitstrings should dominate the histogram?\n"
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Answer\">\n"
"00 and 11 should dominate.\n"
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Why wouldn't a perfect simulator always have the exact same number of 00 counts as 11 counts?\n"
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Answer\">\n"
"Even though a simulator is \"perfect\" and leads to a perfect Bell state, it is still simulating an inherently random process, so statistical fluctuations will still occur. It's like flipping a coin 1000 times: even if there is an exact 50-50 chance of the coin landing on heads or tails, that doesn't mean you'll always get exactly 500 instances of heads and 500 of tails.\n"
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Why might a real quantum computer show some 01 or 10 results even though the simulator didn't?\n"
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Answer\">\n"
"Because real devices have noise. Gates and measurements are not perfect, and that can introduce occasional errors.\n"
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"What is one practical difference between simulators and real quantum computers besides noise?\n"
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Answer\">\n"
"Quantum computers can involve queue time, limited availability, and device-specific constraints that affect how circuits run.\n"
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
